<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · Sacred · c. 1534 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Coventry Carol</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A sad old English Christmas song from a play once acted in the streets of Coventry, sung by mothers to their babies.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous (oldest known setting written out by Thomas Mawdyke, 1591)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the craft guilds of Coventry and their mystery plays</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Coventry, England</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>16th century; text dated 14 March 1534, music dated 13 May 1591</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament · Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional; the oldest known text written down by Robert Croo in 1534, the music by Thomas Mawdyke in 1591)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coventry-carol">Coventry Carol</a>&quot; is the second of three songs in the Pageant of the Shearmen and Tailors, a nativity play that was one of the Coventry Mystery Plays, performed by the city's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>. The pageant tells the Christmas story from the second chapter of the Gospel of Matthew. The carol is sung by three women of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, who come on stage with their children just after an angel has warned Joseph to take his family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>; it is a lullaby of the mothers whose children are about to die in the Massacre of the Innocents, when Herod ordered all boys under two in Bethlehem to be killed. Its author is unknown. Coventry's guild pageants are recorded from 1392 onwards, but the date of the text is not known.</p><p>The only text of the pageant and its songs was edited by Robert Croo (or Crowe), who dated his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> 14 March 1534. Croo worked for some years as a manager of the city pageants: over twenty years he was paid for playing God in the Drapers' Pageant, for making and mending costumes and props, for new dialogue and for copying out the Shearmen and Tailors' Pageant &quot;newly correcte&quot;. He seems to have adapted older material and added verse of his own. The music was added to his manuscript later by Thomas Mawdyke, whose additions are dated 13 May 1591. Mawdyke wrote it in three parts (alto, tenor and baritone), which shows that the &quot;mothers&quot; were played by men, as was usual in the mystery plays; whether he composed the music is debated, and its style may point to an earlier date. The setting contains a well-known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picardy">Picardy</a> third and a striking false relation. Mawdyke, perhaps a tailor of St Michael's parish in Coventry, probably wrote it down for an attempt to revive the plays in the summer of 1591, which the city authorities in the end did not support.</p><p>Religious changes led to the suppression of the plays in the later 16th century, but Croo's prompt book survived. The Coventry antiquarian Thomas Sharp published a transcription in 1817 in his study of the city's mystery plays, and a second edition with the songs' music in 1825. Both were meant as facsimiles, copying Croo's spelling and layout. This proved fortunate: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, by then in the Birmingham Free Library, was destroyed in a fire there in 1879, so Sharp's transcriptions are the only source.</p><p>Although the plays were performed in summer, the lullaby is now sung as a Christmas carol. It reached a wide audience at Christmas 1940, shortly after the Bombing of Coventry in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, when the BBC's Empire Broadcast ended with the carol sung in the bombed-out ruins of the cathedral. The surviving pageants have been revived in the cathedral since 1951. Modern settings have been written by Kenneth Leighton, Philip Stopford and Michael McGlynn, and a four-part setting of the tune by Walford Davies.</p><p>A different tune, the &quot;Appalachian&quot; variant, was collected by the folklorist John Jacob <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Niles</a> in Gatlinburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>, in June 1934, by his account from an old woman who wished to stay anonymous. It has a largely similar text with an added verse. It has not been found elsewhere, and there is reason to think that Niles wrote it himself; Joel Cohen, however, found an 18th-century shape-note song with some of the carol's words and a tune somewhat like Niles's, and argued that the variant is likely to be authentic.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A lullaby sung by mothers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a> to their infant sons, who are about to be killed on Herod's orders. It is a quiet, sorrowful lament in which the cradle song and the grief of the mothers are one.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">English</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">English</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Lully, lulla, þᵂ littell tiné child,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lully, lulla, you little tiny child,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>By by, lully, lullay</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bye bye, lully, lullay,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>þᵂ littell tyné child,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you little tiny child,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>By by, lully, lullay!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bye bye, lully, lullay!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O sisters too, how may we do</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O sisters two, what can we do</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For to preserve þⁱˢ day</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to preserve this day</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>This pore yongling for whom we do singe</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>this poor little one for whom we sing</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>By by, lully, lullay?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bye bye, lully, lullay?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Herod, the king, in his raging,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Herod the king, in his raging,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Chargid he hath this day</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>has given orders this day</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>His men of might in his owne sight</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to his men of might, in his own sight,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>All yonge children to slay,—</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to slay all young children,—</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That wo is me, pore child, for thee,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>So that I am full of woe, poor child, for you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And ever morne and say</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and ever mourn and say,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For thi parting nether say nor singe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for your parting, neither speak nor sing</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>By by, lully, lullay.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bye bye, lully, lullay.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional; Robert Croo's text of 1534 in Thomas Sharp's transcription (1825), as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coventry_Carol" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (5)</summary><ul><li>Sharp's 'þᵂ' is 'thou', 'þⁱˢ' 'this'; 'lully, lulla, lullay' and 'by by' are lullaby sounds, kept.</li><li>2.1: 'too' is generally read as 'two' (the mothers of the pageant).</li><li>2.3: 'yongling', a young one.</li><li>4.1: 'That wo is me' read as 'so that woe is me'.</li><li>4.3: the sense is uncertain: perhaps 'at your parting I can neither speak nor sing &quot;bye bye, lully, lullay&quot;'.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
